"In-region inference, open models, and new European infrastructure for sovereign AI. Mistral announced the strengthening of AI sovereignty through the launch of regional inference endpoints in Europe and the US, the introduction of a priority tier, expanded support for open models, and the establishment of long-term computing infrastructure. Officially launched 'Mistral Regional Endpoints', allowing users to select Europe or the US as the inference execution region to meet data sovereignty and regulatory compliance requirements. Introduced 'Mistral Priority Tier' in public preview for mission-critical workloads, guaranteeing uptime SLAs and custom rate limits. Expanded support for third-party open models on the Mistral platform starting with Z.ai's GLM-5.2 in addition to its own models, and is pursuing plans to secure up to 1 GW of European AI computing infrastructure by 2030. Mistral x HUMAIN Mistral and HUMAIN have entered into a strategic partnership valued at hundreds of millions of euros to strengthen sovereign AI capabilities in Saudi Arabia and the Middle East region. Mistral and HUMAIN announced a strategic collaboration spanning AI infrastructure, advanced model development, and solution deployment, agreeing to focus initially on cybersecurity and voice domains. The two entities will co-develop frontier models with high Arabic language performance, while Mistral explores utilizing HUMAIN's data center infrastructure to meet local computing demands. They will pursue a joint go-to-market strategy targeting regulated industries such as finance, manufacturing, telecommunications, and the public sector to provide sovereign AI solutions where clients retain full control over their data and models. Agentic Search. More accurate and efficient results from your AI systems. Mistral AI has launched 'Agentic Search,' a multi-step retrieval layer that precisely searches and verifies information within complex enterprise documents, significantly improving search accuracy and efficiency. Provides a multi-step search loop to safely navigate and verify sensitive enterprise data across complex, lengthy documents or isolated on-premises and cloud environments. Optimizes search steps by leveraging five tools based on existing search indexes: search, open, navigate, read, and grep. Significantly enhances retrieval accuracy through a precise exploration approach while drastically reducing latency and token consumption. Introducing Shieldstral. Mistral AI has released Shieldstral, a 3B open-weight multimodal safety classification model capable of assessing the safety of text and images using only natural language policy inputs without retraining. By reframing content moderation as a policy-adaptive question answering (QA) task, natural language safety policies can be applied at inference time without retraining. It unifies text and image safety evaluations into a single interface and runs efficiently on a single 16GB NVIDIA GPU. Across text safety and multimodal moderation benchmarks, it achieves performance on par with or exceeding existing open guardrail models up to seven times its size. Leanstral 1.5: Proof Abundance for All Mistral AI has released Leanstral 1.5, an open-source formal proof model that significantly enhances performance in formal verification and real-world code verification. It operates with 6B active parameters out of 119B total parameters, and is released fully open-source under the Apache-2.0 license via Hugging Face and a free API. It underwent a three-stage training approach: intermediate training, supervised fine-tuning (SFT), and CISPO-based reinforcement learning (multi-turn and code agent environments). It achieved state-of-the-art performance by saturating miniF2F, solving 587/672 PutnamBench problems, reaching 87% on FATE-H and 34% on FATE-X, and uncovering 5 previously unknown bugs across 57 tested open-source repositories. UC Berkeley Researchers Release CUA-Lite, an Open Platform Unifying Sandboxes, Data, Evaluation and RL for Computer-Use Agents UC Berkeley researchers have unveiled CUA-Lite, a unified platform that integrates sandboxes, data, evaluation, and reinforcement learning environments for computer-use agents into a single system. It unifies previously fragmented CUA environments, agents, traces, and training frameworks across desktop, browser, and mobile domains into a single interface and schema. It replaces the legacy QEMU/KVM virtual machine-based OSWorld with Lite.OSWorld, a lightweight version running in standard Docker containers without /dev/kvm, significantly reducing memory usage and boosting parallel execution efficiency by approximately 4.6x. Through LiteSample, a unified data schema, it harmonizes more than 10 existing datasets and supports evaluation, SFT, and RL across diverse models and 15+ benchmark environments with a single command. Perplexity Details Its GPU Embedding Stack: How Ivy, Tulip and ROSE Serve pplx-embed Perplexity disclosed its GPU embedding infrastructure architecture designed to efficiently serve pplx-embed and search ranking models. Rather than building a dedicated embedding engine from scratch, it reused the prefill and decode kernels of existing LLM stacks tailored to the characteristics of batch embeddings and online queries. Request processing is decoupled into three services: the Rust-based gateway Ivy, the gRPC scheduling server Tulip, and the Python-based inference engine ROSE. To reduce CPU-side kernel launch overhead occurring at small batch sizes, it aggressively adopted CUDA Graphs to capture the entire model into a single driver call. The Readers' Rebellion An article arguing that text generated by LLMs damages reader trust and provokes backlash, and thus should be used as an editing tool rather than for writing. Writing is a process of testing and refining thought, and LLM-written text violates the social contract between author and reader. Developer surveys show that a vast majority of readers overwhelmingly prefer the authenticity of human-written text, even if imperfect, over flawless prose. Rather than having LLMs write the finished piece, it is preferable to use them as an auxiliary aid for outlining or editing. grep Beats LSP? Why Coding Agents Ignore More Sophisticated Tools An article analyzing why simple grep is used more often than sophisticated LSP tools in coding agent experiments, along with tool efficiency across different tasks. For simple code location tasks, Claude models predominantly preferred grep over precise LSP tools, and forcing LSP usage actually degraded task success rates. Unlike early LSP tools, providing search results along with source code context significantly improved first-attempt success rates and reduced subsequent file read operations. While LSP is advantageous in codebases with significant text search noise and for reference completeness tasks, grep remains more effective for simple location lookups or editing comments and string literals. AIStats 2027 Questions [D] A post seeking advice on whether to submit a quantitative finance paper to computer science conferences, specifically between AISTATS and ICLR, to satisfy graduation requirements. The author has a quantitative finance paper that was rejected by computer science conferences such as UAI and ICDM, but won a best paper award at a finance conference. To fulfill the department's graduation requirement for a computer science paper publication, the author is contemplating the most suitable venue between AISTATS 2027 and ICLR. The author is asking the community whether the paper fits AISTATS's scope of applications and mathematical rigor requirements, as well as where to find the 2027 LaTeX template. Applying Sliding Window Attention to pretrained LLMs at inference time [P] Introduction to a sliding window attention (SWA) implementation and benchmark results that can be applied to pretrained Hugging Face causal LLMs at inference time without model modification or retraining Implemented a bounded KV cache and ring buffer approach as a reusable inference layer that retains only attention sinks and a recent window instead of maintaining the full KV cache Experimental results on Qwen2.5-7B confirmed significant savings in cache memory as context grows and reduced TPOT latency at 16K; however, there is a trade-off where performance degradation may occur on tasks requiring information outside the active window Seeking feedback from LLM inference optimization and long-context model researchers, as well as input on model architectures to validate and benchmark failure cases Google Mantis: An Agentic Vulnerability Scanning Harness for Reducing False Positives Google has released Mantis, an open-source AI agent framework that automates the entire process from vulnerability identification to verification, reproduction, and patching to address the high false positive rates and hallucination issues of existing AI code scanning. Instead of simple brute-force file scanning, Mantis analyzes repository history, security fix logs, architecture, and threat models to summarize context into a hierarchical structure, reducing token usage by 85%. It combines various specialized agents—such as critics, reviewers, strategists, and researchers—with a sandboxed reproduction environment to safely verify whether vulnerabilities are real and filter out false positives. Composed of a modular tool suite of over 15 tools, it boosts efficiency by strategically deploying lightweight models for simple classification and high-performance models for complex tasks such as vulnerability reproduction and patch generation. How Figma Uses AI Agents for Security Covers a case study where Figma's security engineering team built AI agents to automate security alert investigations and vulnerability detection, achieving significant results. Figma built AI agents integrated with Panther SIEM, AWS, Okta, GitHub, and more, reducing complex security alert resolution times by about 70% and decreasing on-call pages by 20%. The team incrementally improved system utility using Claude Opus-based agents and a tripartite memory separation: past alerts, playbooks, and learned database structures. The team maintained safety controls, such as setting agent-generated PRs as drafts by default and blocking sensitive data exposure, and shared the key lesson that precision should be improved before recall. Altman Reflects on 'Inadequate Promotion of AI Benefits' as Big Tech Moves to Persuade the Public The AI industry, led by OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, has acknowledged its failure to sufficiently communicate the technology's benefits to the public—triggering a backlash—and is now launching a full-scale effort to win over public sentiment. OpenAI CEO Sam Altman and US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent pointed out that the AI industry failed in communicating the positive value of the technology to the public. As public backlash grows over power and water consumption by data centers as well as job insecurity, the financial sector is warning of this as a major AI investment risk. Altman defended ChatGPT's water consumption by comparing it to almond cultivation, while Silicon Valley has launched a massive, heavily funded campaign to justify the establishment of data centers. OpenAI Surpasses Anthropic for the First Time in 'Code Arena'—'Astra' Takes #1 in Web Development OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra has claimed the top spot for the first time, surpassing Anthropic's Claude on the Arena coding and web development leaderboards. OpenAI's 'GPT-6 Astra (Max)' scored 1,797 points in the 'Code Arena: Web Development' category, surpassing Anthropic's 'Claude Fable 5.1 (Max)' to secure first place in a specialized coding domain for the first time in history. In addition to web development, it swept first place in Data & Analytics, Consumer Products, and Content Creation Tools, while securing second place in Games & Simulations. It was evaluated as achieving both superior performance and cost efficiency while maintaining a price of $40 per million tokens, the same as the latest Claude model. Anthropic Resets Weekly Usage Limits for 'Claude Max' to Counter the 'Astra' Surge Anthropic has unexpectedly reset the weekly usage limits for its top-tier paid plan, 'Claude Max', in a bid to counter OpenAI's 'GPT-6 Astra' offensive, prevent developer churn, and reinforce ecosystem lock-in. Anthropic carried out a blanket reset of weekly usage limits for all 'Claude Max' subscribers in celebration of the release of its new model 'Fable 5.1' and the U.S. Labor Day weekend holiday. The move is analyzed as an aggressive lock-in strategy to defend its market share in coding and software development against the surging popularity of OpenAI's 'GPT-6 Astra'. The user and developer communities reacted very positively to the measure, which allows them to code and test models without restrictions over the long holiday weekend. GPT-6 Astra Becomes First AI to Achieve a Perfect Score on the CSAT, Sweeping All Four Inquiry Subjects OpenAI's next-generation AI model, GPT-6 Astra, has become the first AI to achieve a completely perfect score across all questions of the 2026 College Scholastic Ability Test (CSAT), including four inquiry subjects. On the '2026 CSAT LLM Solver Dashboard' based on an automated evaluation system developed by student Koo Yu-gyeom, GPT-6 Astra scored a perfect 450 out of 450 points, marking the first perfect score by an AI. Unlike previous models that made errors in certain inquiry subjects, it swept Korean, all optional math subjects, English, Korean History, and four high-difficulty inquiry subjects (Physics 1, Chemistry 1, Life Science 1, and Society & Culture). Its computational efficiency is outstanding, requiring only 130,000 output tokens including the reasoning process, recording significantly lower token usage compared to non-reasoning and competitor models. OpenAI 'Astra', Powerful Security and Safety Features as Well... Looking into the 7 Key Features It covers the advanced cyber capabilities of OpenAI's latest AI model 'GPT-6 Astra' and the corresponding seven key security and safety enhancement measures. OpenAI announced that 'GPT-6 Astra' is the first model whose cybersecurity capabilities have reached the 'Critical' level under its risk management standard, the 'Preparedness Framework'. Its robustness against jailbreak attacks has significantly improved compared to the previous model GPT-5.6 Sol, and refusal criteria for high-risk users have been conservatively expanded. OpenAI suppressed the risks of abuse and misalignment through strict isolation, checkpoint encryption, comprehensive monitoring of execution paths including Chain of Thought (CoT), and enhanced alignment evaluations. Search Gmarket Products with ChatGPT... Launches Plugin Service Gmarket has become the first major open market platform in South Korea to launch a shopping-dedicated plugin service that supports everything from product discovery and recommendations to purchase links through conversations with ChatGPT. By explaining purchasing purpose, budget, target recipient, and more in a conversation with ChatGPT rather than searching for exact product names, users receive recommendations for Gmarket products that match their criteria and are directed to the purchase page. This is the first case among major domestic open market platforms to directly provide ChatGPT-based product discovery and recommendation features. It offers sellers a new AI-powered product exposure channel, and plans are in place to improve recommendation accuracy and expand product categories based on future usage patterns."